# A call into a family runs one of its overrides: `Handler.handle` and `Handler.log` each run their own body or
# `ClickHandler`'s, on the Handler the caller calls them on. What the caller hands the base's method is what the override
# is handed: `handle` an Event, `log` a Click, each a value from beyond. The base's own body (the dispatch's own arrow)
# reads the value it is handed, not the Handler. Each result, a string, goes back to the dispatch that ran it.

route events.handlers.Handler
step Handler()/Handler.handle
step Handler()/Handler.handle/ClickHandler.handle
step Handler()/Handler.log
step Handler()/Handler.log/ClickHandler.log

line entry > Handler()/Handler.handle call
line entry > Handler()/Handler.log call
line Handler > Handler()/Handler.handle/ClickHandler.handle value
line Handler > Handler()/Handler.log/ClickHandler.log value
known #126 line Event·e > Handler()/Handler.handle value                  # handle's own body reads e.name
line Event·e > Handler()/Handler.handle/ClickHandler.handle value
known #126 line Click·e > Handler()/Handler.log value
line Click·e > Handler()/Handler.log/ClickHandler.log value
line Handler()/Handler.handle/ClickHandler.handle > Handler()/Handler.handle back
line Handler()/Handler.log/ClickHandler.log > Handler()/Handler.log back

home Handler Handler()
home Event·e Handler()
home Click·e Handler()

side entry left
